  "prediction": "**Summary of Recent News**\n\n**Internet Archive and Wayback Machine**\n\nAlexa Internet has been donating its crawl data to the Internet Archive since 1996. The data, which are added to the Wayback Machine after an embargo period, provide a valuable resource for researchers and historians.\n\n**London School of Economics (LSE) Incident**\n\nThe LSE is investigating allegations of a Nazi-themed drinking game that led to a brawl involving a Jewish student. The incident occurred during a skiing trip to Val d'Isère, organized by the students' union, which was attended by 150 students from the university's athletics union. According to reports, the game involved playing cards arranged in the shape of a swastika, with players required to \"salute the Führer.\" A Jewish student who objected to the game was subsequently assaulted, suffering a broken nose.\n\nThe LSE has condemned the incident, stating that it does not tolerate antisemitism or any other form of racism. The university's Jewish society has also spoken out against the incident, with its president, Jay Stoll, saying that Nazi glorification and antisemitism have no place in universities.\n\n**Context and Reactions**\n\nThe incident has sparked widespread condemnation, with many students and staff at the LSE expressing shock and outrage. The university's students' union has also condemned the incident, with its general secretary, Alex Peters-Day, saying that it is \"so at odds with everything the LSE and the union stand for.\"\n\nThe incident has also been compared to a similar incident at Oxford University, where members of the Conservative Association were accused of singing an antisemitic song with Nazi references.\n\n**Key Quotes**\n\n* \"Nazi glorification and antisemitism have no place in our universities, which should remain safe spaces for all students.\" - Jay Stoll, president of the LSE's Jewish Society\n* \"This is so at odds with everything the LSE and the union stand for.\" - Alex Peters-Day, general secretary of the students' union\n* \"The athletics union prides itself on our open and tolerant nature and behaviour of this sort is not acceptable and is not an accurate representation of the behaviour we uphold ourselves to.\" - Brendan Mycock, president of the LSE athletics union",
